{
  "platform_id": "2Sz40WqBg8E",
  "profile": {
    "video_id": "kenji:2Sz40WqBg8E",
    "purpose": "Recipe demonstration for a spatchcocked roast chicken coated in herb mayonnaise, cooked to temperature with a predictive thermometer and served with a quick jus.",
    "video_type": "how_to",
    "thesis": "Slathering a spatchcocked chicken in herb mayo keeps the herbs on the bird and improves browning, and spatchcocking lets breast and legs finish at the same time.",
    "key_takeaways": [
      "Spatchcocking makes the breast the thickest part, so legs and breast finish together, and it cooks in about 70% of the usual time.",
      "Mayo stays on the meat better than herb butter, holds the herbs in place, browns well because of its egg proteins, and protects sugary marinades from burning.",
      "Scallions in an herb butter or herb mayo intensify the allium flavor, a technique credited to Charles Kelsey at Cook's Illustrated.",
      "The chicken is roasted at 425°F and pulled when the breast reaches 145°F. Carryover takes it to about 150–155°F, which is pasteurized when held long enough.",
      "With an accurate predictive thermometer, dry brining is unnecessary.",
      "The quick jus is made from the backbone, vegetables, herbs and stock, then finished with lemon, a touch of soy sauce and butter."
    ],
    "outline": [
      {
        "title": "Introduction to roast chicken",
        "summary": "Cold open, then the plan to roast a mayo-slathered chicken. Kenji tells the story of Kevin Bacon praising his mayo turkey recipe on Jimmy Fallon's show.",
        "first_segment": 0,
        "last_segment": 1
      },
      {
        "title": "Preparing the chicken",
        "summary": "Introduces the Combustion Inc. predictive thermometer as a stand-in for brining. Kenji spatchcocks the chicken with poultry shears and explains why spatchcocking evens out the cooking of breast and legs.",
        "first_segment": 1,
        "last_segment": 5
      },
      {
        "title": "Making the herb mayo",
        "summary": "Herb mayo is blended from parsley, sage, rosemary, thyme, garlic, MSG, salt, pepper, lemon zest and scallions. Kenji explains why mayo beats herb butter and how it prevents marinades from burning, with a Microplane tip and a hand blender accident story along the way.",
        "first_segment": 5,
        "last_segment": 12
      },
      {
        "title": "Applying marinade and roasting",
        "summary": "The chicken is coated in the mayo and the probe inserted. It roasts at 425°F with the alarm set for 145°F, followed by an explanation of time-and-temperature pasteurization.",
        "first_segment": 13,
        "last_segment": 16
      },
      {
        "title": "Making a quick pan sauce",
        "summary": "A light jus is started from the chopped backbone, onion, celery and carrot, lightly browned and then simmered with herbs and store-bought chicken stock.",
        "first_segment": 16,
        "last_segment": 19
      },
      {
        "title": "Resting and finishing sauce",
        "summary": "The chicken comes out at 145°F in the breast, with the thigh at 182°F, and rests. The jus is strained and finished with lemon, soy sauce and butter.",
        "first_segment": 20,
        "last_segment": 22
      },
      {
        "title": "Carving and tasting",
        "summary": "Carving the legs and breast. Notes on thickening options (roux, Wondra, cornstarch), on breast versus thigh, and a tasting that shows how little of the herb mixture dripped off.",
        "first_segment": 23,
        "last_segment": 28
      }
    ],
    "passages": [
      {
        "role": "intro",
        "first_segment": 0,
        "starts_with": "Look at how nice and brown",
        "last_segment": 1,
        "ends_with": "makes me zero degrees from Kevin Bacon.",
        "note": "Cold open teaser, greeting, and the Kevin Bacon / Jimmy Fallon mayo turkey anecdote"
      },
      {
        "role": "aside",
        "first_segment": 9,
        "starts_with": "Also a misnomer because I I've",
        "last_segment": 10,
